---
// Architecture principles, on a dark panel.
---
<section class="section wrap" id="approach">
  <div class="sec-head"><p class="kicker">Principles</p><h2>How I Approach Architecture</h2></div>
  <ol class="principles">
    <li class="principle lead">
      <span class="p-num">01</span>
      <div><h3>Business-first Architecture</h3><p>Start with business workflows and domain capabilities before selecting technologies.</p></div>
    </li>
    <li class="principle">
      <span class="p-num">02</span>
      <div><h3>Clear Service Boundaries</h3><p>Define services around business responsibilities rather than simply splitting functionality into technical layers.</p></div>
    </li>
    <li class="principle">
      <span class="p-num">03</span>
      <div><h3>Reliable Distributed Processing</h3><p>Design asynchronous workflows with retries, idempotency, failure handling and observability.</p></div>
    </li>
    <li class="principle">
      <span class="p-num">04</span>
      <div><h3>Data Consistency</h3><p>Treat transactional integrity, concurrency and data correctness as first-class architectural concerns.</p></div>
    </li>
    <li class="principle">
      <span class="p-num">05</span>
      <div><h3>API &amp; Integration Contracts</h3><p>Design explicit contracts with validation, versioning, error handling and backward compatibility.</p></div>
    </li>
    <li class="principle">
      <span class="p-num">06</span>
      <div><h3>Production-minded Engineering</h3><p>Architecture decisions consider deployment, monitoring, security, performance, disaster recovery and operational support from the beginning.</p></div>
    </li>
    <li class="principle">
      <span class="p-num">07</span>
      <div><h3>Pragmatic Technology Choices</h3><p>Use the simplest architecture that satisfies scalability, maintainability and business requirements.</p></div>
    </li>
  </ol>
</section>
